  "dom_id": "step-q78860",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=0$ and $k=3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(0,3).$",
